45417] [17592186347990 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347990 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da7d86ef-4dea-5ade-8600-60a413066868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{x}^{3}y-{y}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af353571-e5f3-5cb8-87db-52892f4fbe38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348265 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ of the rectangular solid if the base is a square with the side of $a=3$ and the height is $h=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f0e792d-8d7d-5fba-9784-ec8ac9d90e29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348271 #edb/ref 1074 "The surface area of the given rectangular solid is $S=2{a}^{2}+4ah.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(a,h) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂S}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =(4a+4h)\\,\\text{d}a+4a\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=3$ and $h=7,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(3,7) & =(4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7)\\,\\text{d}a+4\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01cbcb4d-5667-5e3e-806c-ef99a5773f2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348292 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428484 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428484 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This 